\title{Bilinear Pairings, BLS Signatures, and the Quantifier Boundary of Nondegeneracy}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-30}

\begin{document}
\begin{abstract}
Bilinear pairings are the algebraic foundation of some of the most compact constructions in public-key cryptography: signatures consisting of a single group element, aggregate signatures whose size is independent of the number of signers, and the Menezes--Okamoto--Vanstone (MOV) reduction translating elliptic-curve discrete logarithms into finite-field discrete logarithms. We develop the theory of pairings from a minimal axiomatic core---biadditivity into a multiplicative target group---and show that this single property suffices to derive completeness of the Boneh--Lynn--Shacham (BLS) signature scheme, completeness and compression of aggregate BLS, and the MOV reduction with an \emph{exact} faithfulness statement. Adding a single nondegeneracy hypothesis yields the binding/uniqueness property and a tight, deterministic reduction showing that existential forgery of BLS is equivalent to solving Computational Diffie--Hellman (CDH). We construct a concrete instance of the abstract interface: the determinant form $e((a,b),(c,d)) = \zeta^{\,ad-bc}$ on the rank-two torsion module $(\Z/n\Z)^2$, the coordinate model of the Weil pairing, and prove it is alternating and nondegenerate. Finally we isolate a structural boundary: nondegeneracy \emph{as a bilinear form} (quantified over all partners) and nondegeneracy \emph{against a fixed generator} (quantified over one partner) are governed by different quantifiers, and the alternating law preserves the first while destroying the second, giving a one-line algebraic obstruction to symmetric single-group binding.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
A \emph{bilinear pairing} is a map $e : G \times G \to T$ from an additive abelian group $G$ to a multiplicative abelian group $T$ that is additive in each argument. The canonical instances are the Weil and Tate pairings on the torsion subgroup of an elliptic curve, mapping into the group of roots of unity of a finite field. The construction of these pairings is analytically substantial, but the cryptographic protocols that \emph{consume} pairings never use anything beyond bilinearity and, for soundness, nondegeneracy. We therefore separate the algebra from the analysis: we axiomatize the pairing by its characteristic property, derive every downstream guarantee from it, and then exhibit a concrete instance proving the axioms (including nondegeneracy) are non-vacuous.

\section{Preliminaries}
Throughout, $G$ is an additive abelian group (the elliptic-curve point group, with secret keys acting by scalar multiplication $x\cdot g$), and $T$ is a multiplicative abelian group (the group $\mu_r$ of $r$-th roots of unity). We write $1$ for the identity of $T$ and $0$ for the identity of $G$.

\section{The pairing interface and its bilinear calculus}
\begin{definition}[Pairing]
A \emph{pairing} from $G$ to $T$ is a map $e : G \times G \to T$ such that for all $a,b,p,q\in G$,
\[ e(a+b,q) = e(a,q)\,e(b,q), \qquad e(p,a+b) = e(p,a)\,e(p,b). \]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Degenerate identities]
$e(0,q) = 1$ and $e(p,0) = 1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Setting $a=b=0$ gives $e(0,q) = e(0,q)^2$; cancellation in the group $T$ yields $e(0,q)=1$. The second identity is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Scalar laws]
For $n\in\N$, $e(n\cdot p, q) = e(p,q)^n$ and $e(p, n\cdot q) = e(p,q)^n$; hence $e(a\cdot p, b\cdot q) = e(p,q)^{ab}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$, with base case the previous lemma and inductive step the additivity axiom. The bilinear form combines the two single-slot laws and $(x^a)^b = x^{ab}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Inverse and $\Z$-grading]
If $G$ is a group then $e(-p,q) = e(p,q)^{-1}$, and for $n\in\Z$, $e(n\cdot p, q) = e(p,q)^n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
From $e(p,q)\,e(-p,q) = e(0,q) = 1$. The graded law splits $n$ into $\pm m$ with $m\in\N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Sum--product law]\label{lem:sumprod}
For a finite index set $s$ and $f : s\to G$,
\[ e\!\Big(\sum_{i\in s} f(i),\, q\Big) = \prod_{i\in s} e(f(i),q). \]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction over the finite set: empty case is the degenerate identity, insertion step is additivity.
\end{proof}

\section{BLS signatures: completeness and aggregation}
Public parameters fix a generator $g$. A signer holds secret $x\in\N$ and publishes $X = x\cdot g$. To sign a message with hash $H$, the signer outputs $\sigma = x\cdot H$; a verifier accepts iff $e(\sigma,g) = e(H,X)$.

\begin{theorem}[Completeness]
$e(x\cdot H, g) = e(H, x\cdot g)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both sides equal $e(H,g)^x$ by the scalar laws.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Aggregate compression]
For a finite signer set $s$,
\[ e\!\Big(\sum_{i\in s} x_i\cdot H_i,\, g\Big) = \prod_{i\in s} e(H_i,\, x_i\cdot g). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:sumprod} and then completeness factor-by-factor.
\end{proof}
The verified left-hand object is a single group element $\sigma_{\text{agg}} = \sum_i \sigma_i$, of size independent of $|s|$: pairings yield \emph{short} aggregate signatures.

\begin{theorem}[Batch verification]
If $e(\sigma_i, g) = e(H_i, X_i)$ for each $i\in s$, then $\prod_{i} e(\sigma_i,g) = \prod_i e(H_i,X_i)$.
\end{theorem}

\section{The MOV reduction}
\begin{theorem}[MOV map]
$e(x\cdot g, g) = e(g,g)^x$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{theorem}[Faithfulness]
For $a,b\in\N$, $\;e(a\cdot g, g) = e(b\cdot g, g) \iff a \equiv b \pmod{\ord(e(g,g))}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both sides are powers of $e(g,g)$; equality of powers is congruence of exponents modulo the order.
\end{proof}
\begin{corollary}[Exact recovery]
If $\ord(e(g,g)) \ge n$ and $0\le a,b<n$, then $e(a\cdot g,g) = e(b\cdot g,g)$ implies $a=b$.
\end{corollary}
This is why small embedding degree breaks a curve: a target-group DLP solver returns the secret modulo $\ord(e(g,g))$, exact once that order dominates $\ord(g)$.

\section{Binding and unforgeability under CDH}
\begin{definition}[Left nondegeneracy against $g$]
The pairing is nondegenerate against $g$ if $e(a,g)=1$ implies $a=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Binding / uniqueness]
Under nondegeneracy against $g$, the equation $e(\sigma,g) = e(H, x\cdot g)$ has the unique solution $\sigma = x\cdot H$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Rewrite as $e(\sigma,g) = e(x\cdot H, g)$, so $e(\sigma - x\cdot H, g) = 1$; nondegeneracy forces $\sigma = x\cdot H$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[CDH solution]
Given $g$, $A = a\cdot g$, $B = b\cdot g$, a CDH solution is $S = (ab)\cdot g$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[A forgery is a CDH solution]\label{thm:forge}
If $H = c\cdot g$ and $X = x\cdot g$, any $\sigma$ with $e(\sigma,g) = e(c\cdot g,\, x\cdot g)$ satisfies $\sigma = (xc)\cdot g$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Binding gives $\sigma = x\cdot(c\cdot g) = (xc)\cdot g$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Black-box reduction]
If $\mathrm{adv}(A,B)$ always passes $e(\mathrm{adv}(A,B),g) = e(B,A)$, then $\mathrm{adv}(a\cdot g, b\cdot g)$ is a correct CDH solution for every instance.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Unforgeability under CDH]
If no group element equals the DH value of $(g, a\cdot g, b\cdot g)$, then no $\sigma$ satisfies $e(\sigma, g) = e(b\cdot g,\, a\cdot g)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Contrapositive of Theorem~\ref{thm:forge}.
\end{proof}
The reduction is deterministic and tight, and uses only nondegeneracy against the fixed generator---its scope is exactly one partner.

\section{A concrete nondegenerate alternating pairing}
\begin{definition}[Determinant form]
On $\mathrm{Tor}(n) := (\Z/n\Z)^2$, set $\wdet((a,b),(c,d)) = ad-bc$. The pairing into $T \cong \mu_n$ is $e(p,q) = \zeta^{\,\wdet(p,q)}$, with $\zeta$ the generator corresponding to the additive element $1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Bilinearity]
$e$ is a pairing.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$\wdet$ is additive in each argument by distributivity; the exponential turns sums into products.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Alternating]
$e(p,p) = 1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$\wdet((a,b),(a,b)) = ab-ba = 0$.
\end{proof}
Hence $e(q,p) = e(p,q)^{-1}$, from expanding $1 = e(p+q,p+q)$ and cancelling self-pairings.

\begin{theorem}[Full nondegeneracy]
If $e(p,q) = 1$ for every $q$, then $p=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
With $p=(a,b)$: pairing against $(0,1)$ gives $a=0$, against $(1,0)$ gives $-b=0$. Both coordinates vanish.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Image generator]
$e((1,0),(0,1)) = \zeta^1$, of additive order $n$; the pairing surjects onto $\mu_n$.
\end{proposition}
By full nondegeneracy and binding, the concrete pairing separates points, making every conditional result above unconditional for this explicit pairing.

\section{The quantifier boundary}
\begin{theorem}[Fixed-slot degeneracy]
Let $e$ be alternating on $G$ and $g\ne 0$. Then there is a nonzero $a$ with $e(a,g)=1$, namely $a=g$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from $e(g,g)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Impossibility of symmetric single-group binding]
No alternating pairing on a single group is nondegenerate against any fixed nonzero generator; hence such a pairing cannot supply the binding hypothesis used in BLS unforgeability.
\end{corollary}

Two notions share the name \emph{nondegeneracy} but differ in quantifier: as a form, \emph{for all} $q$, $e(p,q)=1 \Rightarrow p=0$ (which holds); against a fixed $g$, \emph{for one} $g$, $e(a,g)=1 \Rightarrow a=0$ (which fails, witnessed by $a=g$). The alternating law $e(g,g)=1$ is itself the degeneracy certificate. Secure deployments therefore require an asymmetric pairing $e : G_1\times G_2\to T$ with independent groups, making precise the folklore preference for type-3 pairings.

\paragraph{Rogue-key boundary.} Naive same-message aggregation is insecure: against honest key $X_1$, an adversary registers $X_2 = (w\cdot g) - X_1$ and outputs $\sigma = w\cdot H$; then $X_1+X_2 = w\cdot g$ telescopes and $e(\sigma,g) = e(H, X_1+X_2)$ verifies with no honest secret. The defense is distinct messages, keeping per-signer factors separate so aggregate agreement is equivalent to all individual agreements.

\section{A worked numerical example}
In the determinant model with $n=23$ and $g=(1,0)$, take secret $x=9$, so $X=(9,0)$, and hash $H=(5,8)$. The signature is $\sigma = 9\cdot(5,8) = (22,3)$. Verification gives $e(\sigma,g) = 22\cdot0 - 3\cdot1 = -3 \equiv 20$ and $e(H,X) = 5\cdot0 - 8\cdot9 = -72 \equiv 20 \pmod{23}$, so it accepts; tampering to $\sigma'=(22,4)$ yields $-4\equiv 19\ne 20$ and is rejected. For aggregation with $n=29$, four signers with secrets $(4,11,7,24)$ and hashes $((2,3),(5,1),(8,6),(9,9))$ produce the single aggregate point $(16,20)$, whose pairing exponent $-20\equiv 9$ equals $-\sum_i H_{i,2}x_i = -281 \equiv 9 \pmod{29}$. For the rogue-key attack with $n=31$, honest key $X_1=(13,0)$, shared hash $H=(6,5)$, and $w=20$ give rogue key $X_2=(7,0)$ with $X_1+X_2=(20,0)$; the forged $\sigma=20\cdot(6,5)=(27,7)$ satisfies $e(\sigma,g)=-7\equiv 24 = e(H,X_1+X_2)$, a forgery using no honest secret. Finally with $n=13$, only $(0,0)$ pairs trivially against all of $(\Z/13\Z)^2$, yet $g=(3,7)$ satisfies $e(g,g)=21-21=0$, exhibiting fixed-slot degeneracy of a nonzero generator.

\section{Future work}
Three directions stand out. (1) \emph{Symmetric pairings cannot bind a fixed key}: conjecturally every alternating pairing on a single finite abelian group into a cyclic target has, for every nonzero generator, a nonzero trivially-pairing partner---turning deployed folklore into an impossibility theorem. (2) \emph{Uniqueness of the determinant form}: conjecturally every nondegenerate alternating pairing on $(\Z/n\Z)^2$ into $\mu_n$ equals the determinant pairing precomposed with a source automorphism, for a unique primitive root---so the space of such pairings is a torsor under source automorphisms. (3) \emph{Full-order self-pairing characterizes broken curves}: for a non-alternating pairing on a prime-order group, the discrete log transports faithfully iff the self-pairing of the generator has order equal to the group size.

\section{Conclusion}
From a single biadditivity axiom we obtained the complete bilinear calculus, BLS completeness, aggregate compression, the MOV reduction with exact faithfulness, and a tight deterministic forgery-to-CDH reduction. A concrete determinant pairing on $(\Z/n\Z)^2$ realizes the interface and proves its nondegeneracy. The quantifier boundary between the two senses of nondegeneracy---preserved and destroyed by the alternating law---explains in one line why secure pairing-based signatures must be asymmetric.

\end{document}

